package Vixen.Shaders.PostFx

import Vixen.Shaders.Core

// Vignette, chromatic aberration and film grain: the lens-imperfection pass.
//
// One shader for the three because they are always applied together, at the very end, and each is a few
// instructions — three separate fullscreen passes would cost three times the bandwidth to save nothing.
// Each is behind its own `[Permutation]`, so a variant that wants only grain emits only grain.
//
// All three are deliberately *display-referred*: they run after tonemapping, because they model the
// camera and the film rather than the scene. Grain applied in HDR, in particular, is invisible in
// shadows and enormous in highlights.

shader Vignette {
    [Permutation] val UseVignette: bool = true
    [Permutation] val UseChromaticAberration: bool = true
    [Permutation] val UseGrain: bool = true

    /// Whether the image is warped radially before anything else reads it.
    ///
    /// ⚠ First, and it has to be: distortion moves where a pixel *comes from*, so everything after it
    /// samples the warped coordinate and stays registered with the image. Applied last it would warp
    /// the vignette and the grain along with the picture, which is a lens that distorts its own dirt.
    [Permutation] val UseLensDistortion: bool = false

    /// Whether grain scales with darkness, as real film does.
    [Permutation] val LuminanceWeightedGrain: bool = true

    /// Whether a triangular-PDF noise of about one code is added just before the encode.
    ///
    /// ⚠ **Last, and the ordering is the one thing here that can be silently wrong.** Lens distortion
    /// runs first because it moves where a pixel comes from; this runs after the tonemap, after the
    /// vignette and after the grain, because the quantisation step it exists to break up is the one
    /// the *final encode* makes. Dithering before a curve dithers the wrong quantity: the curve
    /// stretches the noise where it is steep and swallows it where it is flat, so the banding
    /// survives in exactly the shallow gradients that had it.
    ///
    /// ⚠ **Not grain, and the difference is not a matter of degree.** Grain is a look — it has an
    /// intensity an artist dials and it is weighted by luminance. This is a quantisation fix of a
    /// fixed size: one code of the target, whatever the target is. A frame that turns grain up does
    /// not stop needing it.
    [Permutation] val UseDither: bool = false

    /// Whether the target the dither is sized for carries the sRGB transfer curve.
    ///
    /// ⚠ **A permutation and not a uniform, because a host knows its own attachment format and the
    /// branch is the same for every pixel of the draw.** `VignetteRenderer` sets it from
    /// `PixelFormat.IsSrgb`, so the variant follows the target rather than an author's belief about
    /// it.
    ///
    /// ⚠ **And it is the difference between a dither and noise, not a refinement of one.**
    /// `ditherLevels` divides the value this shader *outputs*, which is one stored code only where
    /// there is no curve between the two. Against an `Rgba8UNormSrgb` attachment the hardware
    /// encodes on the way out, so a stored code is a linear step whose size changes across the
    /// range — about `0.00016` at `L = 0.01` and about `0.0075` at `L = 0.9`, a factor of
    /// forty-five. A fixed `1/255` is therefore far too much noise in the shadows, which is exactly
    /// where banding is most visible, and too little in the highlights. See `DitherStep`, which
    /// divides by the curve's local slope instead.
    [Permutation] val SrgbTarget: bool = false

    var source: Texture2D
    var linearSampler: Sampler

    /// 0 is no darkening, 1 is fully dark at the corners.
    var vignetteIntensity: float = 0.4f

    /// How abrupt the falloff is. Higher keeps the centre clear for longer.
    var vignetteSmoothness: float = 0.5f

    /// What colour the corners go towards. Black is the ordinary answer.
    var vignetteColor: float3 = float3(0f, 0f, 0f)

    /// Where the vignette is centred, in UV. The screen's middle is (0.5, 0.5).
    var vignetteCenter: float2 = float2(0.5f, 0.5f)

    /// 0 follows the aspect ratio, 1 is a circle whatever shape the screen is.
    var vignetteRoundness: float = 0f

    /// The aspect ratio the vignette is stretched by when `vignetteRoundness` is zero.
    var aspectRatio: float = 1.7777778f

    /// How far the image is pushed out (positive) or pulled in (negative) at the edges.
    var distortionIntensity: float = 0f

    /// Per-axis multipliers, so distortion can be disabled on one axis by setting it to zero.
    var distortionScale: float2 = float2(1f, 1f)

    /// Where the distortion is centred, in UV.
    var distortionCenter: float2 = float2(0.5f, 0.5f)

    /// A zoom applied after the warp, to hide the border a positive distortion pulls in from.
    var distortionZoom: float = 1f

    /// Aberration offset at the screen edge, in UV units.
    var aberrationStrength: float = 0.003f

    var grainIntensity: float = 0.04f
    var grainScale: float = 1f

    /// Advanced by the host per frame, so the grain is not a static pattern.
    var frameIndex: float = 0f

    var screenSize: float2 = float2(1920f, 1080f)

    /// How many steps the target quantises to — 255 for the usual eight-bit encode.
    ///
    /// ⚠ The dither's whole size is one of these, so a value that does not match the swap chain is
    /// either noise nobody asked for or a dither too small to cross a step. It is a uniform rather
    /// than a constant because a ten-bit output is a different number and not a different shader.
    ///
    /// ⚠ **It counts codes of the *stored* value, and turning that into a step of the value this
    /// shader outputs is `SrgbTarget`'s job.** On a linear target the two are the same number. On an
    /// `Rgba8UNormSrgb` one the hardware encodes on the way out, so a stored code is a linear step
    /// that varies across the range, and `DitherStep` divides by the curve's local slope to recover
    /// it. This number is the count either way; what it means in linear units is not.
    var ditherLevels: float = 255f

    /// How many codes of noise, peak to peak either side. One is the value the arithmetic is for.
    ///
    /// ⚠ Below one the noise cannot always carry a value across the step it sits next to, which is
    /// the banding surviving at reduced contrast; above one it is visible as noise in flat areas.
    var ditherIntensity: float = 1f

    stream var uv: float2

    [VertexShader]
    [Semantic("SV_Position")]
    func Vertex([Semantic("SV_VertexID")] vertexIndex: int): float4 {
        uv = Fullscreen.Uv(vertexIndex)
        return Fullscreen.Position(vertexIndex)
    }

    [FragmentShader]
    [Semantic("SV_Target")]
    func Fragment(): float4 {
        // ⚠ `sampleUv` and not `uv` from here on. Everything that reads the source has to read the
        // distorted coordinate or the warp only moves some of the image.
        var sampleUv = uv

        if (UseLensDistortion) {
            sampleUv = Distorted(uv)
        }

        val centred = sampleUv - 0.5f
        var color = UseChromaticAberration ? Aberrated(sampleUv, centred) : source.Sample(linearSampler, sampleUv).rgb

        if (UseVignette) {
            color = lerp(vignetteColor, color, VignetteAmount(sampleUv))
        }

        if (UseGrain) {
            color = Grained(color)
        }

        // ⚠ After the grain and after everything else, because it is about the encode and not about
        // the picture — see `UseDither`.
        if (UseDither) {
            color = Dithered(color)
        }

        return float4(color, 1f)
    }

    /// Samples the three channels at slightly different radii.
    ///
    /// Radial rather than a fixed offset, and scaled by the squared distance from centre: a real lens
    /// has no aberration on axis and increasingly more toward the edge. A uniform offset looks like a
    /// misregistered print instead.
    func Aberrated(at: float2, centred: float2): float3 {
        val amount = dot(centred, centred) * aberrationStrength

        val r = source.Sample(linearSampler, at - centred * amount).r
        val g = source.Sample(linearSampler, at).g
        val b = source.Sample(linearSampler, at + centred * amount).b
        return float3(r, g, b)
    }

    /// How much of the image survives at this point: 1 in the clear centre, 0 in a dark corner.
    ///
    /// Measured from `vignetteCenter` rather than from the middle of the screen, and stretched by the
    /// aspect ratio unless `vignetteRoundness` says otherwise — a circular vignette on a 16:9 screen
    /// darkens the sides long before the top, which is right for a lens and wrong for a framing device.
    func VignetteAmount(at: float2): float {
        val stretch = lerp(aspectRatio, 1f, saturate(vignetteRoundness))

        var offset = at - vignetteCenter
        offset.x = offset.x * stretch

        // ⚠ Normalised by the corner *this* stretch produces, so `distance` is 1 at a corner whatever
        // the roundness is. The constant here used to be sqrt(2), which is that normalisation for a
        // stretch of exactly 1 — the circular case — and the default roundness is 0, which stretches
        // by the aspect ratio instead. On 16:9 that put the whole of both side edges past 1, and
        // everything past 1 is `vignetteColor` outright: an intensity of 0.4 blacked out a third of
        // the frame. The picture it gives is a bright ellipse touching the top and bottom edges,
        // which reads as a lens rather than as a bug, so it survived a long time.
        val corner = 0.5f * sqrt(stretch * stretch + 1f)
        val distance = length(offset) / max(corner, Const.Epsilon)

        val inner = 1f - saturate(vignetteIntensity)
        return smoothstep(1f, inner + (1f - vignetteSmoothness) * 0.5f, distance)
    }

    /// Brown's radial distortion, which is one term and the shape every lens has.
    ///
    /// ⚠ It returns where to *sample from*, so a positive intensity — barrel — reads the image from
    /// nearer the centre and pushes the picture outward. Getting that backwards gives a warp that
    /// looks plausible and is the wrong sign, which nothing downstream can tell you.
    ///
    /// `distortionZoom` exists because a barrel warp pulls the frame in from outside the source, where
    /// there are no texels: without it the border is whatever the sampler clamps, smeared.
    func Distorted(at: float2): float2 {
        var offset = (at - distortionCenter) / max(distortionZoom, Const.Epsilon)
        val radius = dot(offset, offset)
        val warp = 1f + radius * distortionIntensity

        offset = float2(offset.x * lerp(1f, warp, distortionScale.x), offset.y * lerp(1f, warp, distortionScale.y))
        return offset + distortionCenter
    }

    /// Adds a triangular-PDF noise of `ditherIntensity` codes, so the encode's step stops being a band.
    ///
    /// **What a dither actually does is trade amplitude for density.** An eight-bit encode of a
    /// shallow gradient rounds a long run of pixels to one code and then steps: the eye finds the
    /// step, because a straight edge in a smooth field is the one thing it is best at. Adding noise
    /// of about the size of a step makes each pixel round up with a probability equal to how far
    /// through the step it really was, so a run that is a quarter of the way up comes out a quarter
    /// covered — the value survives in the *density* of the higher code rather than in a code that
    /// does not exist.
    ///
    /// ⚠ **Triangular and not uniform, which is what makes it unbiased in the second moment too.**
    /// A single uniform sample of one code makes the error's *variance* depend on where in the step
    /// the value sat — the noise modulates with the signal, which is audible as hiss following the
    /// music in the sound version of this and visible as noise that swells and fades across a
    /// gradient here. The difference of two independent uniforms is triangular over ±1 code and
    /// leaves both the mean and the variance of the error independent of the input, which is the
    /// standard result and the reason it is two hashes rather than one.
    ///
    /// ⚠ **Static, deliberately: no `frameIndex`.** The banding is spatial and a still frame has it,
    /// so a pattern that changes every frame would add crawl to a still image to fix something a
    /// fixed pattern fixes. Grain is the one that moves, and it moves because film does.
    ///
    /// Per channel rather than one number for all three: a common offset is a luminance dither, which
    /// leaves the banding in any gradient that changes hue rather than brightness.
    ///
    /// ⚠ **The amplitude is `DitherStep`'s and not `1/levels`.** On a target with a transfer curve
    /// one stored code is not a fixed step of the value written here, and a dither of the wrong size
    /// is noise in the highlights and a band that survives in the shadows. See `SrgbTarget`.
    func Dithered(color: float3): float3 {
        val pixel = uv * screenSize
        val x = uint(int(pixel.x))
        val y = uint(int(pixel.y))

        val low = float3(
            Random.Float01(Random.Hash3(x, y, 0u)),
            Random.Float01(Random.Hash3(x, y, 1u)),
            Random.Float01(Random.Hash3(x, y, 2u))
        )

        val high = float3(
            Random.Float01(Random.Hash3(x, y, 3u)),
            Random.Float01(Random.Hash3(x, y, 4u)),
            Random.Float01(Random.Hash3(x, y, 5u))
        )

        val step = float3(DitherStep(color.x), DitherStep(color.y), DitherStep(color.z))
        return color + (low - high) * step
    }

    /// How much of *this shader's* value one stored code of the target is, at a value.
    ///
    /// ⚠ **The whole of what makes a dither a dither rather than noise, and it is a property of the
    /// target and not of the picture.** A dither has to be the size of the step it is breaking up.
    /// `ditherLevels` counts the steps the *stored* value has; on a linear target one of those is
    /// `1/levels` of what this shader writes, which is what the arithmetic assumed. On an sRGB
    /// target it is not: the encode is steep near black and shallow near white, so one code is a
    /// linear step of about `0.00016` at `L = 0.01` and about `0.0075` at `L = 0.9`.
    ///
    /// The correction is one division by `dE/dL`. For the curve's upper segment
    /// `E = 1.055·L^(1/2.4) − 0.055`, so `dE/dL = (1.055/2.4)·L^(1/2.4 − 1)` and the step is
    /// `(1/levels)·2.4·L^(1 − 1/2.4)/1.055`. Below `0.0031308` the standard's own linear segment
    /// takes over and the slope is the constant `12.92` — ⚠ **which is not a rounding detail**: the
    /// power form has an infinite slope at zero, so a shader that used it everywhere would divide by
    /// something arbitrarily small and put a step of unbounded size on the darkest texels in the
    /// frame.
    ///
    /// ⚠ Per channel, because `L` is per channel. A single step computed from a luminance would be
    /// the wrong size on any pixel that is not grey, which is the same reason the noise itself is
    /// three hashes and not one.
    func DitherStep(value: float): float {
        val code = ditherIntensity / max(ditherLevels, Const.Epsilon)

        if (SrgbTarget) {
            val linear = max(value, 0f)

            if (linear <= 0.0031308f) {
                return code / 12.92f
            }

            return code * 2.4f * pow(linear, 1f - 1f / 2.4f) / 1.055f
        }

        return code
    }

    /// Adds grain, optionally weighted so shadows are grainier than highlights.
    func Grained(color: float3): float3 {
        val pixel = uv * screenSize / max(grainScale, Const.Epsilon)
        val seed = Random.Hash3(uint(int(pixel.x)), uint(int(pixel.y)), uint(int(frameIndex)))
        val noise = Random.Signed(seed)

        var weight = grainIntensity

        if (LuminanceWeightedGrain) {
            // Real film grain is most visible in mid-tones and shadows, because the emulsion is
            // saturated in highlights. Uniform grain reads as digital noise.
            weight = weight * (1f - Fullscreen.Luminance(color) * 0.7f)
        }

        return max(color + float3(noise * weight), float3(0f))
    }
}
